mmittee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-22","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-09","committee":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Communities Housing and Environment Cabinet Advisory Board","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-11","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Communities Housing and Environment Cabinet Advisory Board","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Tunbridge Wells council starts year with stronger cash position as investment income outperforms","lead":"Tunbridge Wells Cabinet heard on 24 September that the council opened the financial year with \u00a341.9 million in cash, \u00a31.9 million more than budgeted, after capital projects totalling \u00a314.074 million were rescheduled into 2026/27. The stronger liquidity position has boosted investment returns. The council forecasts investment income of \u00a32.2 million for the year against a \u00a32 million budget, a surplus of \u00a3200,000 that will be transferred to reserves, after achieving an average interest rate of 4.13 per cent rather than the budgeted 3.94 per cent. Q1 investment balances reached \u00a348.3 million, well above the year-end budget assumption of \u00a328.9 million. The council holds no external debt. However, the capital programme is forecast to spend \u00a319.9 million in 2026/27 against a budget of \u00a38.9 million\u2014more than double\u2014and the Grant Volatility Reserve, which buffers against swings in government funding, is forecast to be completely depleted by March 2027.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Quarter 1 Treasury Management 202627","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s84985/84985.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-10-05","committee":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board","paper_title":"Budget Update 2027_28","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s85206/85206.pdf","headline":"Council holds no external debt and is maintaining minimum statutory reserves, demonstrating financial discipline.","detail":"The council has not borrowed externally and maintains its Medium-Term Financial Strategy minimum thresholds of \u00a33.0m General Reserves and \u00a31.0m Capital Receipts, both forecast to be met even as total reserves decline. This contrasts with councils carrying significant external debt and gives maximum flexibility in future decisions.","category":"borrowing","matters":"A council with no external debt has maximum financial flexibility and lower refinancing risk; lenders value borrowing restraint and this is a positive resilience indicator.","figures":"General Fund: \u00a33,475k (Mar-27 and Mar-28), declining to \u00a32,674k (Mar-29); both above minimum \u00a33,000k requirement. Capital Receipts maintained at \u00a31,000k minimum.","quote":"The Council does not have any external debt but, the s151 Officer has the flexibility to borrow where appropriate.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-05","committee":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board","paper_title":"Budget Update 2027_28","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s85206/85206.pdf","headline":"Local Government Reorganisation uncertainty is creating material risk to staffing and budget stability.","detail":"The Secretary of State is reviewing the decision to proceed with creating four new unitary councils in Kent. Recruitment and retention of staff during this uncertainty is explicitly identified as a major factor determining the size of the budget deficit, and any reorganisation will require negotiation on how the financial settlement is redistributed.","category":"governance","matters":"Reorganisation creates budget risk through staff turnover costs, service planning disruption, and settlement uncertainty; lenders assess budget stability and this uncertainty undermines forward budget projections.","figures":"Not quantified in the document","quote":"The Secretary of State has decided to review the decision to move forward with the implementation of a four unitary model for Kent.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-05","committee":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board","paper_title":"Budget Update 2027_28","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s85206/85206.pdf","headline":"Government funding settlement declines year-on-year despite being secured for three years.","detail":"The council received a three-year settlement (subject to parliamentary approval), but the annual amounts fall: \u00a36.788m (2027/28) to \u00a36.492m (2028/29). This declining trajectory means increasing structural pressure to reduce services, raise council tax, or find other income.","category":"income","matters":"Declining core government funding forces choices between service cuts, council tax rises, or deficits; a three-year forecast into decline affects both immediate budget options and long-term borrowing appetite.","figures":"Total government grant 2027/28: \u00a36,788,280; 2028/29: \u00a36,491,877 (both including housing grants)","quote":"The Government completed a Fair Funding Assessment of Local Government and have provided a three-year settlement (although Parliamentary approval is still required on an annual basis).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-05","committee":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board","paper_title":"Budget Update 2027_28","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s85206/85206.pdf","headline":"Contract renegotiation is the largest single budget pressure at \u00a34.2m in 2027/28.","detail":"The budget variance analysis shows contracts as a \u00a34.199m pressure in 2027/28, the largest single variance item. This reflects the council's exposure to cost escalation when contracts are renegotiated in an inflationary environment, with Capital Economics forecasting inflation pressures into 2027/28.","category":"demand","matters":"Recurring contract cost inflation that the council cannot control without service cuts or efficiency gains is a structural pressure; a council managing \u00a34m annual contract variances faces recurring deficits unless it finds offsetting savings.","figures":"Contracts variance \u00a34,199k (2027/28 Subjective Variance), the largest line item in the variance table","quote":"The pressures faced by the council by virtue of operating in one of the most expensive parts of the country combined with very low unemployment levels is further compounded when external contracts are reprocured.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-05","committee":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board","paper_title":"Budget Update 2027_28","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s85206/85206.pdf","headline":"Budget deficit improved dramatically from \u00a34.5m to \u00a3801k but still requires closure.","detail":"The council reported a \u00a34.5m deficit in February 2026, now reduced to \u00a3801k for 2027/28 after major contract awards and the Local Government Finance Settlement. Directors are continuing work to close this remaining gap through service planning and proposals for efficiencies, with officers to report back in December with draft proposals.","category":"overspend","matters":"A council demonstrating material improvement in its deficit position reassures lenders about budget discipline; the remaining \u00a3801k gap still requires action but the trajectory is in the right direction.","figures":"\u00a3801,000 deficit for 2027/28, down from \u00a34.5m reported in February 2026","quote":"A budget deficit of \u00a3801,000 is currently forecast for 2027/28, a decrease on the \u00a34.5m reported to Full Council in February following the awarding of major contracts and notification of the Local Government Finance Settlement.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Tunbridge Wells","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-26","since_iso":"2026-08-27","distress":false,"headline":"Successor unitary authority will open on 1 April 2028 with net usable reserves of \u00a31,150k.","lead":"The opening financial position of a new unitary authority directly determines its financial resilience and capacity to absorb unexpected costs or service pressures during establishment.","also":["38 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board on 2026-10-05","n_findings":39,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix D Usable Reserves Q1 202627","top_paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s85000/85000.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":158,"news_drafts_total":1}}